What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The definition of whole body oxygen delivery is the rate at which oxygen is supplied to all tissues in the body. Oxygen delivery is essential for the production of energy and the maintenance of various metabolic processes in the body. When oxygen delivery is insufficient, the body may struggle to function efficiently, leading to metabolic stress and potentially tissue damage.

Oxygen delivery is influenced by several factors, including oxygen uptake, blood volume, and blood efficiency in delivering oxygen.

The correct oxygen delivery calculation is:

Oxygen Delivery (ODO) = Oxygen uptake (VOO) + Oxygen consumption (VOCO)

Oxygen uptake (VOO) is the oxygen absorbed into the blood from the lungs. Oxygen consumption (VOCO) is the oxygen used by the body.

The correct answer is: Oxygen delivery (ODO) = oxygen uptake (VOO) + oxygen consumption (VOCO).",relevance,"# Task Description
