"If the loading dose of phenytoin in children is 20 mg/kg of body weight to be infused at a rate of 0.5 mg/kg/min, over how many minutes should the dose be administered to a 32-lb. child?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
If the loading dose of phenytoin in children is 20 mg/kg of body weight to be infused at a rate of 0.5 mg/kg/min, over how many minutes should the dose be administered to a 32-lb. child?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the time it takes to administer the loading dose, we first need to convert the body weight from pounds to kilograms.

1. **Convert pounds to kilograms:**  
   \( 1 \text{ lb} = 0.4536 \text{ kg} \)  
   \( 32 \text{ lb} \times 0.4536 \text{ kg/lb} = 14.54 \text{ kg} \)  

2. **Calculate the loading dose:**  
   \( 20 \text{ mg/kg} \times 14.54 \text{ kg} = 290.8 \text{ mg} \)  

3. **Calculate the infusion time:**  
   \( 0.5 \text{ mg/kg/min} \times 14.54 \text{ kg} = 7.27 \text{ mg/min} \)  
   \( \text{Time} = \frac{290.8 \text{ mg}}{7.27 \text{ mg/min}} = 40.1 \text{ min} \)  

The loading dose should be administered in approximately \(\boxed{40 \text{ minutes}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
