<USER_REQUEST>
A solution has a hydronium ion concentration \([H_3O^+]\) of \(10^{-4}\) M. What is the nature of this solution (acidic, basic, or neutral)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The concentration of hydonium ions \([H_3O^+]\) is given as \(10^{-4}\) M. The concentration of hydroxide ions \([OH^-]\) can be calculated using the ion product of water (\(K_w\)):  
\[
K_w = [H_3O^+][OH^-] = 10^{-14} \text{ at 25°C}
\]  
Rearranging for \([OH^-]\):  
\[
[OH^-] = \frac{K_w}{[H_3O^+]} = \frac{10^{-14}}{10^{-4}} = 10^{-10} \text{ M}
\]  
Since \([H_3O^+] > [OH^-]\), the solution is acidic.  

Thus, the correct answer is \(\boxed{\text{acidic}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

