\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{4\pi}{3} \frac{e^2}{4\pi \epsilon_0} \frac{1}{r} \left(1 - \frac{z^2}{4\pi \epsilon_0} \frac{1}{r} \right)
\]

where:
- \( z_1 = +2 \) (charge of Ca²⁺ ion)
- \( z_2 = -1 \) (charge of Cl⁻ ion)
- \( r = 0.231 \, \text{nm} \) (distance between the ions)
- \( \epsilon_0 = 8.854 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{F/m} \) (permittivity of free space)
- \( e = 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \) (elementary charge)

The Born-Ledefler equation is:

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{4\pi}{3} \frac{e^2}{4\pi \epsilon_0} \frac{z_1 z_2}{r}
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{4\pi}{3} \times \frac{(1.602 \times 10^{-19})^2}{8.854 \times 10^{-12}} \times \frac{2 \times (-1)}{0.231 \times 10^{-9}}
\]

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{4\pi}{3} \times 2.18 \times 10^{-21} \, \text{J} \times \frac{-2}{0.231 \times 10^{-9}}
\]

\[
\text{Latt Energy} = \frac{4\pi}{3} \times 2.18 \times 10^{-21} \times \frac{-2}{0.231 \times 10^{-9}} = \frac{4\pi}{3} \times 2.18 \times 10^{-21} \times (-8.67 \times 10^9)
\]

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = \frac{4\pi}{3} \times (-1.89 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{J})
\]

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = -8.1 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{J}
\]

The negative sign indicates that the energy is negative, which means it is a negative enthalpy (enthalpic term), and thus the lattice energy is positive. The magnitude is:

\[
\text{Lattice Energy} = 8.1 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the lattice energy of CaCl₂ is \(\boxed{8.1 \, \text{J}}\).
