In summary, the stereoselectivity factor \( s \) is a measure of the relative rate of homochiral dimer formation versus mixed dimer formation. A higher \( s \) leads to a higher e.e, and a higher amplification of the optical asymmetry. Conversely, a lower \( s \) leads to a lower e.e, and less amplification of the optical asymmetry.
