","The equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by the point-point form of the equation of a line:  
\[ y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a). \]  
This equation is derived from the definition of the derivative as the slope of the tangent line at a point. The derivative \( f'(a) \) is the slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \), and the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is on the tangent line.  

Thus, the equation of the tangent line is:  
\[ y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a). \]  

This is the standard form of the equation of the tangent line to the graph of \( f \) at \( (a, f(a)) \).  

Final Answer:  
