","To prove that \( B \subseteq A \) if \( A \triangle B \subseteq A \), we proceed by contradiction.

Assume that \( B \notsubset A \), which means there exists some \( b \in B \) such that \( b \neq a \) for all \( a \in A \). Thus, \( b \notin A \).

Since \( A \triangle B = (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A) \), we have \( A \triangle B \subseteq A \). This implies that \( (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A) \subseteq A \).

Since \( b \in B \), we consider the two cases:

1. If \( b \in A \setminus B \), then \( b \in A \) and \( b \neq A \), which contradicts the fact that \( b \notin A \).
2. If \( b \in B \setminus A \), then \( b \in B \) and \( b \notin A \). This is consistent with the assumption \( b \neq a \) for all \( a \in A \), so \( b \notin A \).

Since \( A \triangleright B \subseteq A \), we have \( (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A) \subseteq A \). Since \( b \in B \setminus A \), we must have \( b \in A \), which contradicts the fact that \( b \notin A \).

Thus, the assumption \( B \notsubset A \) leads to a contradiction. Therefore, \( B \subseteq A \).

