","The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\frac{1}{\lambda \lambda_0} \left(1 - \left(1 - \frac{\lambda^2}{\lambda_0^2}\right)^{1/2}\right),
\]
where \(\lambda_0 = \frac{c}{n_f}\) is the wavelength in the free-of-field (FOT) medium, and \(\lambda\) is the wavelength in the guided mode. 

For a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\) cladding, the decay length is given by:
\[
\frac{1}{\lambda \lambda_0} \left(1 - \left(1 - \frac{\lambda^2}{\lambda_0^2}\right)^{1/2}\right) = \frac{1}{\lambda \left(1 - \left(1 - \frac{\lambda^2}{\lambda_0^2}\right)^{1/2}\right)},
\]
where \(\lambda_0 = \frac{c}{n_f}\). 

The decay length is given by:
\[
\text{Decay Length} = \frac{1}{k_e},
\]
where \(k_e\) is the evanescent wave wave number. 

The evanescent wave wave number is given by:
\[
k_e = \sqrt{k_0^2 - k_g^2},
\]
where \(k_0 = \frac{c}{\lambda}\) is the free-wave number and \(k_g\) is the guided wave number. 

The decay length is:
\[
\text{Decise Length} = \frac{1}{k_e} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{k_0^2 - k_g^2}}.
\]

The decay length is also given by:
\[
\text{Decay Length} = \frac{1}{k_e} = \frac{1}{k_0 \sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{k_g}{k_0}\right)^2}} = \frac{1}{k_0 \sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{n_g}{n_0}\right}^2)},
\]
where \(n_g\) is the group index of the guided mode and \(n_0\) is the refractive index of the free-of-field medium. 

The decay length is:
\[
\text{Decade Length} = \frac{1}{\lambda \lambda_0} \left(1 - \left(1 - \frac{\lambda^2}{\lambda_0^2}\right)^{1/2}\right) = \frac{1}{\lambda \left(1 - \left(1 - \frac{\lambda^2}{\lambda_0^2}\right)^{1/2}\right)}.
\]

The given condition is \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the guided mode and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the free-of-field medium. 

For GaAs, \(n \approx 1.12\). For Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{}_{0.8}\), \(n \approx 1.25\). 

