","To show that the Douford–Frankkel method is stable for any \(\Delta t\), we analyze the stability condition for the scheme. The scheme is:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n+1} - \phi_j^{n-1} + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
Rearranging the equation, we get:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_j^{n+1} = \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n + \phi_{j-1}^n \right) + \phi_j^{n-1}.
\]
This can be rewritten as:
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{\2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) \phi_j^{n+1} = \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n + \phi_{j-1}^n \right) + \phi_j^{n-1}.
\]
Let \( \lambda = \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \). Then the equation becomes:
\[
(1 + \lambda) \phi_j^{n+1} = 2\lambda \left( \phi_{j+1}^n + \phi_{j-1}^n \right) + \phi_j^{n-1}.
\]
Solving for \(\phi_j^{n+1}\):
\[
