- \(q\) is the electron charge (\(1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C}\)),
- \(\epsilon_s\) is the oxide dielectric constant (\(3.9 \times 10^{-28} \text{ F/cm}\)),
- \(\phi_s\) is the surface potential,
- \(N_d\) is the donor doping concentration,
- \(N_i\) is the intrinsic carrier concentration.

The shift in threshold voltage due to \(V_{BS}\) is given by:

\[
\Delta V_{T} = \frac{q N_d V_{BS}}{\epsilon_s}
\]

Given:
- \(\Delta V_{T} = -1.0 \text{ V}\),
- \(N_d = 10^{16} \text{ cm}^{-3}\),
- \(\epsilon_s = 3.9 \times 10^{-28} \text{ F/cm}\),
- \(q = 1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C}\).

Substitute the values into the formula:

\[
-1.0 = \frac{(1.6 \times 10^{-19})(10^{16})(V_{BS})}{3.9 \times 10^{-28}}
\]

Solve for \(V_{BS}\):

