</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is determined by the uncertainty in the measurement. The lifetime of the particle is related to the width of the energy level in the energy spectrum due to the Heisenberg uncertainty principle. The uncertainty in the energy \(\Delta E\) is inversely proportional to the lifetime \(T_{1/2}\):

\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar}{2T_{1/2}}
\]

where \(T_{1/2}\) is the lifetime and \(T_{1/2} = 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s. The reduced Planck constant \(h\) is \(6.626 \times 10^{-34}\) J·s, and \(\hbar = \frac{h}{4\pi} \approx 1.054 \times 10^{-34}\) J·s.

First, convert the lifetime to seconds: \(T_{1/2} = 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s.

Now, calculate the energy uncertainty:
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J·s}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
\]

Convert the energy uncertainty to MeV:
\[
\Delta E \approx 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} \approx 3.3 \text{ MeV}
\]

The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be within the uncertainty of the measured value. The measured value is 1385 MeV, and the uncertainty is approximately 3.3 MeV. Therefore, the expected range of outcomes is:

\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 3.3 \text{ MeV}
\]

The range of outcomes is:
\[
(1385 - 3.3) \text{ MeV} \leq \text{Measurement} \leq (1385 + 3.3) \text{ MeV}
\]
\[
1381.7 \text{ MeV} \leq \text{Measurement} \leq 1388.3 \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is approximately \(\boxed{1385 \pm 3.3 \text{ MeV}}\).
