An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \):**
   \[
   A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 111.11 \text{ V/V}
   \]

2. **Determine the new open-loop gain \( A_v' \):**
   The gain decreases by 6 dB, which corresponds to a factor of \( 2^{-6} = 0.25 \).
   \[
   A_{vf} = 100 \times 0.25 = 25 \text{ V/V}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \):**
   \[
   A_{vf}' = \frac{A_{vf'}}{1 - m_v} = \frac{25}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{25}{0.9} \approx 27.78 \text{ V/V}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain:**
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{27.78 - 111.11}{111.11} \right) \times 100 \approx -76.5\%
   \]

**Final Answer:**
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{-76.5\%}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
