\]
where \( t \) is the coordinate time, \( x^\nu \) are the spatial coordinates, \( \tau \) is the proper time, and \( \Gamma^{0\nu} \) are the components of the metric tensor. The covariant derivative of a vector \( V^\mu \) is also often written as:
\[
\nabla V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial \tau} + \Gamma^{0\nu} V^\mu \frac{\partial \tau}{\partial x^\nu}
\]
Here, \( \partial_\tau \) is the partial derivative with respect to proper time \( \tau \). 

The covariant derivative is a generalization of the ordinary derivative to curved spacetime. The ordinary derivative \( \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\nu} \) does not preserve the metric tensor \( g_{\mu\nu} \), meaning that:
\[
\frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\nu} \cdot \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\nu} \neq \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\nu} \cdot \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\nu}
\]
This is because the components of \( V^\mu \) are functions of the spatial coordinates \( x^\nu \), and the metric tensor \( g_{\mu\nu} \) is also a function of \( x^\nu \). The covariant derivative accounts for the change in the metric tensor when differentiating the vector components. 

