","The squared four-momentum transfer \( Q^2 \) is defined as the difference between the initial and final four-momenta:

\[
Q^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2
\]

Substituting the given four-momenta:

\[
Q^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2 = \left( \left( \frac{E_f}{c}, \mathbf{p}_f \right) - \left( \frac{E_i}{c}, \mathbf{p}_i \right) \right)^2
\]

Expanding the difference of the four-momenta:

\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f - E_i}{c}, \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right)^2
\]

The square of a four-momentum is the sum of the squared components:

\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f - E_i}{c} \right)^2 + \left( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right) \cdot \left( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right)
\]

The dot product of the vectors simplifies to:

\[
\left( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right) \cdot \left( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right) = \mathbf{p}_f \cdot \mathbf{p}_f - 2 \mathbf{p}_f \cdot \mathbf{p}_i + \mathbf{p}_i \cdot \mathbf{p}_i
\]

Thus, the expression for \( Q^2 \) becomes:

\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f - E_i}{c} \right)^2 + \mathbf{p}_f \cdot \mathbf{p}_f - 2 \mathbf{p}_f \cdot \mathbf{p}_i + \mathbf{p}_i \cdot \mathbf{p}_i
\]

Using the definition of the square of a four-momentum, we can rewrite the dot products in terms of energies and momenta:

\[
\mathbf{p}_f \cdot \mathbf{p}_f = \left( \frac{E_f}{c} \right)^2 - \left( \frac{E_f}{c} \right)^2 = E_f^2
