","The work done by the gas during isothermal expansion is given by:  
\[ W = \int_{V_1}^{V_2} P \, dV \]  
For an ideal gas, the pressure \( P \) is related to the volume \( V \) by the ideal gas law:  
\[ P = \frac{RT}{V} \]  
where \( R \) is the gas constant and \( T \) is the temperature.  

The initial volume is \( V_1 \), and the final volume is \( V_2 = nV_1 \). Substituting \( P \) into the work integral:  
\[ W = \int_{V_1}^{nV_1} \frac{RT}{V} \, dV \]  
\[ W = RT \ln V \bigg|_{V_1}^{nV_1} \]  
\[ W = RT \ln (nV_1) - RT \ln (V_1) \]  
Using the logarithm property \( \ln a - \ln b = \ln \frac{a}{b} \):  
\[ W = RT \ln \left( \frac{nV_1}{V_1} \right) \]  
\[ W = RT \ln n \]  

Thus, the work done is \( \boxed{RT \ln n} \).",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
